From f2d8c986e29e08218659980f88996bf7d9d1d547 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yun Dou <dixyes@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 13:40:16 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] arm64: Add emulation of the LSE atomic instructions

A CPU without FEAT_LSE takes an undefined instruction exception on the
atomic memory operations, the swaps and the compare and swaps, which a
binary built for a newer target can contain. The architecture has no
unprivileged exclusive access, so the operation is made on the task's
behalf with the exclusive load and store the kernel itself uses, and the
exception is not passed on.

The compare and swap is one exclusive sequence: what was read is
compared with the comparand, and the value is stored where they match,
with the sequence taken again while the store keeps failing on the
exclusive monitor. Every other operation is that sequence with the value
it stores computed from what was last read, which is how the kernel
carries out an operation the architecture has no instruction for.

An LSE operation needs a mapping that may be written, which an exclusive
store from the kernel cannot be asked, so what the task may write is
checked first and the fault it would have taken is delivered in its
place.

The number of operations carried out is counted, and can be read from
<debugfs>/lse_emul/stats, together with what the stack pointer of a
task that faulted says about a stack a faster path could use.

Assisted-by: dsh:DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash
(cherry picked from commit 789cd6553197cbb1d05097f43e900eb5032e2de0)
